-- lib/compile/x86ok.cub — lemmas of runs of lib/isa/x86, for the template theorems: a run splits at
-- any step; a store to an address and to another commute. Uses lib/linproof.cub's eqw-sound, weq-sound.
-- Lemmas of runs are proved of a step function that is a variable (run-of): the model is never
-- unfolded but where a statement is about a particular run, and there by computation.
--
-- (2026-11-12, from tracks/E-lemmas.md, now removed) For x86 as a later target, the form the paper
-- fixes (docs/Hyperactive_Final_Paper.pdf, 11 Nov 2026, sha256 e8f358b4cd2c…, §9.3 “An exact decoded machine and a structural compiler theorem”): a block is bytes with, for every
-- frame state meeting its precondition, a finite trace of the model's transition to its exit
-- address meeting its postcondition (Block, eq. (9.12) of that section); blocks compose by seq
-- under prefix stability and relocation (seq, eq. (9.13)); the compiler is the fold of one block per
-- constructor (the compiler as a fold, eq. (9.15), with the relational congruence, eq. (9.16)). The facts about the imported model such proofs need: it
-- reaches memory a byte at a time (application_level_memory's rb_/wb loops, each with the
-- canonical check of the signed address plus offset, the address through sail_mask_signed 64→48,
-- sign-extension to 52, then x86-read-mem); fetch reads the instruction bytes through read_iptr,
-- get_prefixes and add_to_iptr. Under a frame precondition (each region's base a user address with
-- its length as margin, regions separated) these give: fetch returns the block's bytes, a read or
-- write in a region is the plain byte load or store, rip advances by the instruction length.
-- Proofs follow the model's own evaluation step by step, not what normalization happens to
-- compute.
import "../isa/x86/run.cub"

